{"name":"Evergreen Forest","hex":"#0e695f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"evergreen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of plants, especially trees, that do not shed their leaves seasonally.","Continually fresh or self-renewing.\n Being a clause which causes an automatic renewal of a contract unless action is taken.\n Of a document, a piece of software, or a data set: kept continually up to date (as opposed to being published at regular intervals and outdated in the meantime)"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree that does not shed its leaves or needles seasonally.","A conifer tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/evergreen"},{"term":"forest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense uncultivated tract of trees and undergrowth, larger than woods.","Any dense collection or amount."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover an area with trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Evergreen forest","blocks":[{"text":"An evergreen forest is a forest made up of evergreen trees. They occur across a wide range of climatic zones, and include trees such as conifers and holly in cold climates, eucalyptus, live oak, acacias, magnolia, and banksia in more temperate zones, and rainforest trees in tropical zones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Young_Sitka_spruce_plantation_-_geograph.org.uk_-_949091.jpg/330px-Young_Sitka_spruce_plantation_-_geograph.org.uk_-_949091.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evergreen_forest"},{"title":"Evergreen","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, an evergreen is a plant which has foliage that remains green and functional throughout the year. This contrasts with deciduous plants, which lose their foliage completely during the winter or dry season. Consisting of many different species, the unique feature of evergreen plants lends itself to various environments and purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Abies_alba_02.jpg/330px-Abies_alba_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evergreen"},{"title":"Forest","blocks":[{"text":"A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025 found that forests covered 4.14 billion hectares, or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg/500px-%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%94%CF%81%CF%85%CE%BC%CF%8C%CF%82_%CE%A3%CE%B1%CE%BC%CE%B1%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%AC%CF%82.jpg/1280px-%CE%95%CE%B8%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CF%8C%CF%8